# The order in which each layer's fusion may be worked out. One row per layer: `rank` is 0 where
# nothing lies beneath the layer, and NULL where its descent reaches a loop.
#
# An order exists only where nothing expands to itself
#   The precondition that *no name expands to itself* is what guarantees an order exists: each
#   layer comes one step after the deepest thing beneath it. A loop is exactly the case where none
#   exists, which is what "no order exists in which to evaluate the stack" means.
#
# `rank` is the evaluation order, and `depth` is not
#   composition/descent.sqlc's `depth` is per path; `rank` is the deepest over all paths. A layer
#   reachable at depth 1 and at depth 2 must not be worked out until both arrivals are ready, so a
#   walk in depth order can work out a fusion before one of its parts exists. The shallowest and the
#   deepest differ exactly where a layer is reached by paths of two lengths, and checks/jagged_layer
#   is the same thing seen from the sums.
#
# A loop has no order
#   Where a descent reaches a loop there is no deepest arrival, for the layers on it and for every
#   layer above them, so those get NULL, never the depth at which the loop guard happened to stop.
#   checks/layers_move_together reports the loop.
#
# A window, not a second walk
#   composition/descent.sqlc has already done the recursion, so the order is one aggregate over what
#   it reached. Another recursive CTE would work out again what the data already carries.
#
# Carried into two drawings
#   `examples/diagramming` writes it into each filing's lane, and `examples/graphs` writes it into
#   the corpus-wide layer graph's, through `rank/graph_nodes.sqlc`.
#
# An emitter must write the order
#   An emitter that writes none leaves the renderer to draw its own placeholder, and this relation
#   gives either a `rank` of at least 0 or NULL, so a negative one on a page is a value the model
#   cannot produce. Every lane count stays exact through that, which is why it is the emitter's
#   obligation and not a count's.
-- composition/descent.sqlc aggregated to the deepest arrival under each layer.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       CASE WHEN coalesce(bool_or(d.is_cycle), false) THEN NULL
            ELSE coalesce(max(d.depth), 0) END AS rank,
       count(d.filing)           AS arrivals
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) d ON d.root_filing = l.filing AND d.root_layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
